Weekly digest scheduler configuration
=====================================

The weekly digest is driven by `send_digest.py` (run from anywhere that can
reach the database). It emails every user who has "Weekly availability digest"
turned on a list of their currently available books. Pick ONE schedule below.

--- Option A: Render Cron Job ----------------------------------------------

Dashboard -> New -> Cron Job
  Repository        : this repo, branch `phase1-standalone-tbr`
  Build command     : pip install -r requirements.txt
  Run command       : .venv/bin/python send_digest.py
  Schedule (cron)   : 0 9 * * 1                  # 9:00 AM Mondays

Environment (copy from the web service; Render does NOT inherit them):
  DATABASE_URL      <same Postgres URL as the web service>
  RESEND_API_KEY    <key used by the web service>
  EMAIL_FROM        <optional, if a Resend domain is verified>
  SECRET_KEY        <optional; send_digest.py only needs DATABASE_URL + email>

--- Option B: macOS launchd / crontab ---------------------------------------

Add to `crontab -e`:

  0 9 * * 1 cd "/Users/orangeimac/Documents/LibraryAssistant" \
    && DATABASE_URL="<your-postgres-url>" \
       RESEND_API_KEY="<your-resend-key>" \
       ./.venv/bin/python send_digest.py \
       >> /tmp/mynextread_digest.log 2>&1

(Cron runs sh, so the `\` line continuations must be kept on ONE logical line
if your crontab doesn't handle backslashes.)

--- Option C: webhook digest (no DB/email credentials needed) ----------------

A plain HTTP POST can trigger the same digest with just one shared token, so
ANY scheduler can fire it - cron-job.org, GitHub Actions, a Render Cron Job
that curl's it, even a friend's machine. It only needs CRON_TOKEN, which the
web service already has in its environment.

1. Generate the token once and add it to Render -> Environment (web service):
     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
   (the same value goes in the scheduler job below)
2. Render Cron Job (or any HTTP-capable scheduler):
     Run command   : curl -fsS -X POST \
                       -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_TOKEN" \
                       https://libraryassitant.onrender.com/api/system/digest
     Schedule      : 0 9 * * 1
   With cron-job.org, paste the URL and set this request header:
     Authorization: Bearer <token>
3. The endpoint responds 200 with {"digest_sent": N}; wrong or missing
   tokens get 401, and the endpoint is a 404 whenever CRON_TOKEN is unset.

--- Verify ----------------------------------------------------------------

Run once by hand (prints how many emails were sent):

  ./.venv/bin/python send_digest.py            # with the env vars above set

--- Optional: free custom sender domain ------------------------------------

Resend sends "from" onboarding@resend.dev by default (fine for emailing
yourself). To use your own sender address without paying for a domain, get a
free subdomain that lets you add TXT/CNAME records (freedns.afraid.org is the
most reliable; duckdns.org also works):

  1. Sign up at https://freedns.afraid.org and register a free subdomain, e.g.
     mynextread.mooo.com.
  2. In Resend -> Domains -> Add Domain, enter that subdomain. Resend shows
     ~3 records to add (verification TXT, SPF TXT, DKIM CNAME/TXT).
  3. Add each record in afraid.org's control panel (record type TXT or CNAME;
     the target lives under the subdomain itself).
  4. Wait for Resend to verify the domain (usually minutes), then set in
     Render -> Environment and on the cron job/Mac crontab:
       EMAIL_FROM=MyNextRead <me@mynextread.mooo.com>
  5. Save; Render redeploys. "Send test email" in Settings now shows the new
     sender.

(Alternative: skip all this. Since every recipient is your own address,
onboarding@resend.dev is invisible to anyone else. Real domains are ~$10/yr.)